/* ============================================================================
   coin-toss-game.epher -- the expected value of a two-outcome game

   A coin is tossed once. Heads pays you $2 and tails costs you $1, so
   your net gain G is $2 with probability 1/2 and -$1 with probability
   1/2, and the expected value of the game is

     E[G] = 2 * 1/2 + (-1) * 1/2 = 1/2,

   50 cents in your favor every toss. A game is fair when its expected
   value is zero (Ross, "A First Course in Probability", ch. 4): this
   one is not, unless you pay 50 cents for the right to play, which
   shifts the expectation to exactly zero.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/games/coin-toss-game.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\games\coin-toss-game.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/games/coin-toss-game.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- results ----
// The two outcomes and their probabilities, as exact fractions.
print("P(heads, +$2):", 1/2)
print("P(tails, -$1):", 1/2)

// The expected value: 2 * 1/2 - 1 * 1/2 = 1/2.
ev = 2 * (1/2) - 1 * (1/2)
print("expected value of the game:", ev)

// Fairness: an entry fee equal to the expected value makes the game
// fair, because the player's expectation becomes 1/2 - 1/2 = 0.
print("fair entry fee:", 1/2)
print("house expected gain per free toss:", 0 - ev)
/* ---- expected output ----
= P(heads, +$2): 0.5
= P(tails, -$1): 0.5
= 0.5
= expected value of the game: 0.5
= fair entry fee: 0.5
= house expected gain per free toss: -0.5
*/
